            "required": false,
            "type": "text"
          },
          {
            "name": "summary",
            "required": false,
            "type": "text"
          },
          {
            "name": "cover",
            "required": false,
            "type": "image"
          },
          {
            "name": "p1",
            "required": false,
            "type": "text"
          },
          {
            "name": "p2",
            "required": false,
            "type": "text"
          },
          {
            "name": "p3",
            "required": false,
            "type": "text"
          },
          {
            "name": "p4",
            "required": false,
            "type": "text"
          },
          {
            "name": "quote",
            "required": false,
            "type": "text"
          },
          {
            "name": "link",
            "required": false,
            "type": "url"
          }
        ],
        "id": "posts",
        "items": [
          {
            "id": "post-no-dig-bed",
            "slug": "no-dig-bed",
            "status": "published",
            "values": {
              "cover": "/api/assets/5e0cc8ba312d5cd542bd758af3764c1b7c1841a2be5f0c05397981cbf53138cc.svg",
              "date": "2026-11-08",
              "link": "/posts/no-dig-bed",
              "p1": "When we took on plot 14 the far corner was a mat of couch grass, the kind with white roots that snap and grow back twice as many. Digging it out would have taken a month of weekends. Instead I covered it.",
              "p2": "The method is simple. Flatten the weeds, wet them, lay plain brown cardboard with generous overlaps, and spread fifteen centimetres of compost on top. The cardboard starves the grass of light; the worms pull the compost down over winter.",
              "p3": "I used three builders' bags of compost from the council green waste scheme for a bed of about six square metres. It looked absurdly tall in November. By March it had settled to half that height and was dark and crumbly.",
              "p4": "The only work since has been pulling the odd grass shoot that found a gap at the edge. Next year this bed gets onions and garlic, which like firm ground and do not mind a little competition.",
              "quote": "“The cardboard does the weeding while you sleep. You only have to be patient enough to let it.”",
              "summary": "Cardboard, compost and patience: how I turned a patch of couch grass into a bed I can plant in spring, without lifting a spade.",
              "title": "Starting a no-dig bed in late autumn",
              "topic": "Beds and soil",
              "when": "8 November 2026"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "post-frost-and-leeks",
            "slug": "frost-and-leeks",
            "status": "published",
            "values": {
              "cover": "/api/assets/a2349543b826173d1166a146e0337096d7641c80171234c19d3b5c833c7c3fd3.svg",
              "date": "2026-10-19",
              "link": "/posts/frost-and-leeks",
              "p1": "On the second Sunday of October the allotment was white at nine in the morning. I walked up expecting losses: the last courgettes were black, the nasturtiums had collapsed, and the dahlias were finished.",
              "p2": "The leeks looked exactly as they had the week before. If anything they were brighter, the blue-green leaves stiff with frost and standing straight up. By eleven the sun had cleared the hedge and they were dripping and fine.",
              "p3": "Leeks are one of the few crops that are happy to sit in the ground all winter and be picked as you need them. I used to lift them all in November and store them, which meant soft leeks by January. Now I leave them where they are.",
              "p4": "This year we have two varieties, an early one for autumn soups and a hardier one for February, and about sixty plants between them. That should be enough for a leek a week until spring, with some to give away.",
              "quote": "“The plants that look the most delicate in summer are often the ones that cope best with the cold.”",
              "summary": "Our first hard frost came early this year. The leeks shrugged it off, and taught me to stop fussing over the winter beds.",
              "title": "What the frost taught me about leeks",
              "topic": "Winter vegetables",
              "when": "19 October 2026"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "post-saving-bean-seed",
            "slug": "saving-bean-seed",
            "status": "published",
            "values": {
              "cover": "/api/assets/63bd2951a28665106d05880d8e9dba2d785099ca172414f464975c7c9e9e9113.svg",
              "date": "2026-09-27",
              "link": "/posts/saving-bean-seed",
              "p1": "Runner beans and French beans are the easiest seed to save. The flowers mostly pollinate themselves, so the beans you save grow into plants much like their parents, and the seeds are big enough to handle.",
              "p2": "In August I stop picking from the two healthiest plants and let the pods swell. By late September they are dry and papery, and the beans inside rattle. I pick them on a dry afternoon and lay them in a seed tray in the shed for another fortnight.",
              "p3": "Then I shell them at the kitchen table, throw out anything shrivelled or spotted, and put the rest in paper envelopes with the variety and the year written on the front. They keep for three or four years in a cool, dry tin.",
              "p4": "This year I saved about four hundred seeds from a speckled climbing bean an older plot holder gave me. That is far more than I need, so some will go to the seed swap in the village hall in February.",
              "quote": "“Every bean in the tin is a small promise about next summer.”",
              "summary": "A handful of pods left on the plants in August becomes next year's whole crop. Here is how I dry, sort and store them.",
              "title": "Saving seed from this year's beans",
              "topic": "Seeds",
              "when": "27 September 2026"
            }
          }
        ],
        "name": "Posts"
      }
    },
